  "standard": "PC.TRIG.B.1",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "prompt": "A heart monitor trace is modelled by $y = 3\\sin(6x)$, where $x$ is in degrees. How many degrees wide is ONE full beat?",
  "answerType": "numeric",
  "primaryAnswer": "60",
  "alternativeAnswers": [],
  "acceptanceMode": "normalized",
  "hint": "One full beat is one full wave — that is the PERIOD.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "One full beat is one full wave — that is the PERIOD."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "Period $= 360^\\circ \\div b$, and here $b = 6$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$360 \\div 6 = 60$, so one beat is $60^\\circ$ wide."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "On a monitor, the trace repeats the same bump over and over. One repeat is one period.",
    "The number in front of $x$ inside the sine is $6$, so the wave is squeezed six times tighter.",
    "Period $= 360 \\div 6 = 60^\\circ$. The amplitude $3$ tells you how TALL the bump is, not how wide."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Count the beats in one turn",
      "steps": [
        "b = 6 means 6 whole waves fit into 360°.",
        "Share 360° out among 6 beats: 360 ÷ 6 = 60° each."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Follow the inside",
      "steps": [
        "A wave finishes when the inside of the sine has run from 0 all the way to 360.",
        "The inside is 6x, so 6x = 360 gives x = 60. One beat spans 60° of x."
      ]
    }
  ],
